  "text": "**Solution 3 is the only one with a credible final answer, and I'm choosing it with moderate confidence.** I can't check the literature in this environment, so this rests on memory.\n\n- **Solution 1 (c = 1)** conflates the conjectured threshold with a proven theorem. Kahane conjectured that $o(\\log|z|)$ growth forces affineness, and piecewise-linear examples suggest $\\log|z|$ is the natural threshold. That is a conjecture, not the quantitative Beurling–Helson theorem as stated, so it doesn't establish $c = 1$.\n- **Solution 2 (c = 1/2)** cites a claimed improvement over Lebedev's $1/22$, but I don't know of any such result. Its attribution of $1/22$ to Lebedev also conflicts with the usual attribution to Konyagin and Shkredov, so I can't verify it.\n- **Solution 3 (c = 1/22)** matches my recollection of Konyagin and Shkredov's \"A quantitative version of the Beurling–Helson theorem\" (Funct. Anal. Appl., 2015). I recall their bound as $O(\\log^{1/22-\\varepsilon}|z|)$ forcing affineness, and the problem's phrasing \"$\\log^{c-\\varepsilon}$\" fits that form. The conjectured optimum of 1 is consistent with this being the best proven exponent.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
